  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Eid al-Fitr is a major Islamic festival marking the end of Ramadan’s month-long dawn-to-sunset fasting, celebrated on the first three days of Shawwal (the 10th month). It signifies gratitude, spiritual renewal, and community, featuring communal morning prayers, special meals, charity (Zakat al-Fitr), new clothes, and gift-giving (\"Eidi\"). Key Aspects of Eid al-Fitr: Significance: It is the \"Festival of Breaking the Fast,\" celebrating the completion of Ramadan’s spiritual cleansing and self-restraint, providing a time for joy, charity, and family reconnection. When it is Celebrated: It falls on the first day of Shawwal. Because it is based on the lunar calendar, the Gregorian date shifts approximately 11 days earlier each year. Religious Observances: Muslims start the day by attending special congregational prayers in mosques or open areas, often taking a different route home, and paying Zakat al-Fitr (charity for the needy) before the prayer. Traditions & Celebrations: Greetings: Friends and family exchange greetings of \"Eid Mubarak\" (Blessed Eid) or \"Eid Said\" (Happy Eid). Festivities: People wear new or best clothes, decorate homes, and gather with family for large, often sweet-heavy, meals. Charity & Gifts: Children receive \"Eidi\" (money or gifts), and charity is heavily emphasized to ensure the poor can celebrate. Note on Two Eids: Eid al-Fitr: \"Lesser Eid,\" marking the end of Ramadan. Eid al-Adha: \"Greater Eid,\" occurring later in the year, commemorating Prophet Ibrahim's sacrifice.",
